1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,613
It's not garlic or coffee - the real culprits behind your breath might be in your wine glass, your fridge, and your medicine cabinet.

2
00:00:07,981 --> 00:00:16,374
Dry mouth means less saliva, and bacteria multiply fast, breaking proteins into the sulfur compounds behind bad breath.

3
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:20,421
Alcohol, dairy, and allergy meds all speed that up.

4
00:00:20,789 --> 00:00:31,380
Alcohol blocks the hormone that helps your body hold onto water, so your mouth dries out fast - alternate every drink with a glass of water to keep saliva flowing.

5
00:00:31,748 --> 00:00:39,950
Milk and cheese proteins get broken down by bacteria into hydrogen sulfide, a rotten-egg-smelling compound of its own.

6
00:00:40,318 --> 00:00:45,319
Rinse with water or crunch on an apple after dairy to help clear it out.

7
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:54,988
Common antihistamines block the signal that tells your glands to make saliva, and over 400 medications list dry mouth as a side effect.

8
00:00:55,356 --> 00:01:01,202
Keep water close, chew sugar-free gum, and ask your pharmacist about a lower-drying option.
